  "answer_core": "Don’t pick a dissertation direction yet. Spend the next couple of weeks running two evidence‑gathering gates. First, test whether the Silva/data path is real: verify what data exists, who controls access, when you can actually see it, what collaboration terms would look like, and whether someone can cover the single‑cell gaps. Confirm that your advisor explicitly supports exploring this path rather than assuming alignment. Second, keep the fallback path intact: check that your advisor is still willing to resource it, that it remains technically viable, and that you have a clear stop‑date and criteria for walking away from the Silva option if it stalls.\n\nSequence the politics cleanly. Talk to your advisor first. Use Dr. Silva only for targeted, low‑cost checks about data, collaboration depth, and timing. Go to the committee only after you know (a) whether the data path is actually accessible and (b) whether your advisor would support the move without subtly redirecting you back to the fallback.\n\nMake the choice only after both gates give you concrete evidence. The right direction is the one you can actually finish, not the one that sounds most exciting.",
